and temporal characteristics. <source>J. Neurosci.</source> <volume>19</volume>:<fpage>6372</fpage>&#x02013;6384.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10414966</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib36"><citation citation-type="journal">Pozzo-Miller, L.D., N.B. Pivovarova, R.D. Leapman, R.A. Buchanan, T.S. Reese, and S.B. Andrews. <year>1997</year>. Activity-dependent calcium sequestration in dendrites of hippocampal neurons in brain slices. <source>J. Neurosci.</source> <volume>17</volume>:<fpage>8729</fpage>&#x02013;8738.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9348342</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib37"><citation citation-type="journal">Robb-Gaspers, L.D., P. Burnett, G.A. Rutter, R.M. Denton, R. Rizzuto, and A.P. Thomas. <year>1998</year>. Integrating cytosolic calcium signals into mitochondrial metabolic responses. <source>EMBO J.</source> <volume>17</volume>:<fpage>4987</fpage>&#x02013;5000.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9724635</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib38"><citation citation-type="journal">Sala, F., and A. Hern&#x000e1;ndez-Cruz. <year>1990</year>. Calcium diffusion modeling in a spherical neuron. Relevance of buffering properties. <source>Biophys. J.</source> <volume>57</volume>:<fpage>313</fpage>&#x02013;324.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2317553</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib39"><citation citation-type="journal">Schuhmeier, R.P., and W. Melzer. <year>2004</year>. Voltage-dependent Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> fluxes in skeletal myotubes determined using a removal model analysis. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>123</volume>:<fpage>33</fpage>&#x02013;51.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14676283</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib40"><citation citation-type="journal">Sipido, K.R., and W.G. Wier. <year>1991</year>. Flux of Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> across the sarcoplasmic reticulum of guinea-pig cardiac cells during excitation-contraction coupling. <source>J. Physiol.</source> <volume>435</volume>:<fpage>605</fpage>&#x02013;630.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1770453</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib41"><citation citation-type="journal">Strier, D.E., A.C. Ventura, and S.P. Dawson. <year>2003</year>. Saltatory and continuous calcium waves and the rapid buffering approximation. <source>Biophys. J.</source> <volume>85</volume>:<fpage>3575</fpage>&#x02013;3586.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14645051</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib42"><citation citation-type="journal">Suzuki, S., M. Osanai, N. Mitsumoto, T. Akita, K. Narita, H. Kijima, and K. Kuba. <year>2002</year>. Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>-dependent Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> clearance via mitochondrial uptake and plasmalemmal extrusion in frog motor nerve terminals. <source>J. Neurophysiol.</source> <volume>87</volume>:<fpage>1816</fpage>&#x02013;1823.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11929903</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib43"><citation citation-type="journal">Thayer, S.A., and R.J. Miller. <year>1990</year>. Regulation of the intracellular free calcium concentration in single rat dorsal root ganglion neurones in vitro. <source>J. Physiol.</source> <volume>425</volume>:<fpage>85</fpage>&#x02013;115.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2213592</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib44"><citation citation-type="journal">Wanaverbecq, N., S.J. Marsh, M. Al-Qatari, and D.A. Brown. <year>2003</year>. The plasma membrane calcium-ATPase as a major mechanism for intracellular calcium regulation in neurones from the rat superior cervical ganglion. <source>J. Physiol.</source> <volume>550</volume>:<fpage>83</fpage>&#x02013;101.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12879862</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib45"><citation citation-type="journal">Weber, C.R., K.S. Ginsburg, K.D. Philipson, T.R. Shannon, and D.M. Bers. <year>2001</year>. Allosteric regulation of Na/Ca exchange current by cytosolic Ca in intact cardiac myocytes. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>117</volume>:<fpage>119</fpage>&#x02013;131.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11158165</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib46"><citation citation-type="other">Weibel, E.R., and H. Elias. <year>1967</year>. Introduction to stereologic principles. <italic>In</italic> Quantitative Methods in Morphology. E.R. Weibel and H. Elias, editors. Springer-Verlag, Berlin. 89&#x02013;98.</citation></ref><ref id="bib47"><citation citation-type="journal">Wennemuth, G., D.F. Babcock, and B. Hille. <year>2003</year>. Calcium clearance mechanisms of mouse sperm. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>122</volume>:<fpage>115</fpage>&#x02013;128.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12835474</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><app-group><app id="app1"><title>APPENDIX</title><sec><title>Surface Membrane Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> Transport: J<sub>VSCC</sub>, J<sub>Extru</sub></title><p>When Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> uptake and release by mitochondria and the ER are inhibited, the only remaining components of <inline-formula><tex-math id="M47">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\tilde {J}}_{i}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula> represent the net Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> flux across the plasma membrane (<inline-formula><tex-math id="M48">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\tilde {J}}_{PM}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula>). We distinguished two components of <inline-formula><tex-math id="M49">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\tilde {J}}_{PM}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula>: Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> entry through voltage-gated Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> channels (<inline-formula><tex-math id="M50">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\tilde {J}}_{VSCC}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula>), and Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> extrusion (<inline-formula><tex-math id="M51">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\tilde {J}}_{Extru}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula>), representing contributions from all other surface membrane Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> transport pathways, including pumps and leaks. Since these pathways operate in parallel, their contributions to <inline-formula><tex-math id="M52">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\tilde {J}}_{i}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula> are additive. Therefore,<disp-formula id="fda1"><label>(A1)</label><tex-math id="M53">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{gather*}\frac{d[Ca^{2+}]_{i}}{dt}=-\frac{{\tilde {J}}_{{\mathrm{i}}}}{v_{Cell}(f_{i}{\kappa}_{i})} \\=-\frac{{\tilde {J}}_{VSCC}+{\tilde {J}}_{Extru}}{v_{Cell}(f_{i}{\kappa}_{i})}.\end{gather*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>This equation can be recast into a form that is more easily related to measured quantities:<disp-formula id="fda2"><label>(A2)</label><tex-math id="M54">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}\frac{d[Ca^{2+}]_{i}(t)}{dt}=- \left \left[\frac{I_{Ca}(t)}{2Fv_{Cell}f_{i}{\kappa}_{i}}+J_{Extru}\right] \right \end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>using the following relations:<disp-formula id="fda3"><label>(A3)</label><tex-math id="M55">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{gather*}{\tilde {J}}_{VSCC}=\frac{I_{Ca}}{2F} \\J_{Extru}=\frac{{\tilde {J}}_{Extru}}{v_{Cell}(f_{i}{\kappa}_{i})}\end{gather*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>and F is the Faraday constant. We plotted J<sub>Extru</sub> vs. [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>i</sub> and fit with the following equation, which describes a parallel pump/leak system whose overall transport rate depends on [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>i</sub>:<disp-formula id="fda4"><label>(A4)</label><tex-math id="M56">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}J_{Extru}=J_{PMCA}+J_{leak},\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>where<disp-formula id="fda5"><label>(A5)</label><tex-math id="M57">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{gather*}J_{PMCA}=\frac{V_{{\mathrm{max}},PMCA}}{ \left \left(1+ \left \left[\displaystyle\frac{K_{d,PMCA}}{[Ca^{2+}]_{i}}\right] \right ^{n_{PMCA}}\right) \right } \\J_{leak}=k_{leak}([Ca^{2+}]_{i}-[Ca^{2+}]_{o}).\end{gather*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>This equation incorporates the finding that in sympathetic neurons treated with FCCP and Tg, the rate of Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> removal at each point in time during the recovery depends on the [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>i</sub> level at that time. Thus, the time dependence of J<sub>Extru</sub> during the recovery is derived from the time dependence of [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>i</sub>:<disp-formula id="fda6"><label>(A6)</label><tex-math id="M58">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}J_{Extru}(t)=J_{Extru}([Ca^{2+}]_{i}(t)).\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula></p><p>It should be noted that in some cells (e.g., human T cells) Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> extrusion via the PMCA is time dependent (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib3">Bautista et al., 2002</xref>). When fitting <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="fda4">Eqs. A4</xref> and <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="fda5">A5</xref> to J<sub>Extru</sub> measurements during the recovery (as in <xref rid="fig2" ref-type="fig">Fig. 2 C</xref>), we reduced the number of free parameters by requiring that J<sub>Extru</sub> &#x0003d; 0 when [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>i</sub> is at its resting level, making it possible to solve for k<sub>leak</sub> in terms of the remaining parameters.</p></sec><sec><title>Mitochondrial Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> Uniporter: J<sub>uni</sub></title><p>To investigate the contribution from mitochondrial Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> uptake via the uniporter in isolation from Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> release by the mitochondrial Na/Ca exchanger, responses were also elicited in the absence of FCCP, using either Na<sup>&#x0002b;</sup>-free pipette solutions or application of CGP to inhibit mitochondrial Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> release. In this case, <inline-formula><tex-math id="M59">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\tilde {J}}_{i}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula> includes a third component representing the uniporter:<disp-formula id="fda7"><label>(A7)</label><tex-math id="M60">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}\frac{d[Ca^{2+}]_{i}}{dt}=-\frac{{\tilde {J}}_{VSCC}+{\tilde {J}}_{Extru}+{\tilde {J}}_{uni}}{v_{Cell}(f_{i}{\kappa}_{i})}.\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>As in Case 1, this equation can be recast into a form that is more easily related to measured quantities:<disp-formula id="fda8"><label>(A8)</label><tex-math id="M61">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}\frac{d[Ca^{2+}]_{i}}{dt}=- \left \left[\frac{I_{Ca}}{2Fv_{Cell}(f_{i}{\kappa}_{i})}+(J_{Extru}+J_{uni})\right] \right ,\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>where<disp-formula id="fda9"><label>(A9)</label><tex-math id="M62">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}J_{uni}=\frac{{\tilde {J}}_{uni}}{v_{Cell}(f_{i}{\kappa}_{i})}.\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula></p><p>We characterized the sum (J<sub>Extru</sub> &#x0002b; J<sub>uni</sub>) quantitatively as described in <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib7">Colegrove et al. (2000a)</xref>. In brief, this flux was measured by calculating &#x02212;d[Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>i</sub>/dt during the recovery after depolarization-induced [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>i</sub> elevations in Tg-treated cells in the absence of FCCP under conditions where mitochondrial Na/Ca exchange is inhibited. During the recovery, I<sub>Ca</sub> &#x0003d; 0, so that [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>i</sub> changes under the influence of a net flux (J<sub>Extru</sub>&#x0002b;J<sub>uni</sub>) that can be measured directly by calculating &#x02212;d[Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>i</sub>/dt. We plotted (J<sub>Extru</sub>&#x0002b;J<sub>uni</sub>) vs. [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>i</sub> during the recovery and fit with the following equation:<disp-formula id="fda10"><label>(A10)</label><tex-math id="M63">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}\frac{d[Ca^{2+}]_{i}}{dt}=-(J_{PMCA}+J_{leak}+J_{uni}),\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>where J<sub>PMCA</sub> and J<sub>leak</sub> are as described in Case 1 and<disp-formula id="fda11"><label>(A11)</label><tex-math id="M64">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}J_{uni}=\frac{V_{{\mathrm{max}},uni}[Ca^{2+}]_{i}}{ \left \left(1+ \left \left[\displaystyle\frac{K_{d,uni}}{[Ca^{2+}]_{i}}\right] \right ^{n_{uni}}\right) \right },\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>which was approximated by the following equation:<disp-formula id="fda12"><label>(A12)</label><tex-math id="M65">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{gather*}J_{uni}{\cong} \left \left[\frac{V_{{\mathrm{max}},uni}}{K_{d,uni}^{n_{uni}}}\right] \right [Ca^{2+}]_{i}^{n_{uni}+1} \\=A[Ca^{2+}]_{i}^{n_{uni}+1},\end{gather*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>where A is a constant, which is valid as long as [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>i</sub> &#x0226a; K<sub>d,uni</sub>. Based on available information, K<sub>d,uni</sub> &#x0223c; 10&#x02013;20 &#x003bc;M, which is 5&#x02013;10 times the largest measured [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>i</sub> elevation shown in this study. This approximation was used because it was not possible to obtain unique parameters for <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="fda11">Eq. A11</xref> when [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>i</sub> is low compared with K<sub>d.uni</sub>. While <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="fda12">Eq. A12</xref> provided a good description of the [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>i</sub> dependence of J<sub>uni</sub>, the parameter values obtained from different sets of measurements ranged widely (<xref ref-type="table" rid="tbl1">Table I</xref>). This could be due to the high sensitivity of the parameters to subtle differences in the shape of J<sub>uni</sub> and/or a functional dependence of A on n<sub>uni</sub> (see <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="fda12">Eq. A12</xref>).</p><table-wrap position="float" id="tbl1"><label>TABLE I</label><caption><p>Representative Parameter Values</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">Plasma Membrane/Cytoplasm</th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="center">Mitochondria</th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="center">ER</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">k<sub>leak</sub> &#x0003d; 3.7 &#x000d7; 10<sup>&#x02212;7</sup> s<sup>&#x02212;1</sup></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">(A,n<sub>uni</sub>) range: (4 &#x000d7; 10<sup>&#x02212;4</sup>,1)&#x02013;(2 &#x000d7; 10<sup>&#x02212;10</sup>, 3)</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">P<sub>basal</sub> &#x0003d; 0.01 s<sup>&#x02212;1</sup></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">V<sub>max,Extru</sub> &#x0003d; 30 nMs<sup>&#x02212;1</sup></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">(Units of A give nM/s in A12)</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">P<sub>max,RyR</sub> &#x0003d; 0.05 s<sup>&#x02212;1</sup></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">EC<sub>50,Extru</sub> &#x0003d; 350 nM</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left"><inline-formula><tex-math id="M66">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\tilde {V}}_{{\mathrm{max}},NaCa}=4{\raisebox{1mm}{\line(1,0){6}}}5\hspace{.167em}{\mathrm{{\mu}M}}\hspace{.167em}{\mathrm{s}}^{-1}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">EC<sub>50,RyR</sub> &#x0003d; 2.6 &#x003bc;M</td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">n<sub>Extru</sub> &#x0003d; 3.5</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">EC<sub>50,Na/Ca</sub> &#x0003d; 5 &#x003bc;M</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">n<sub>RyR</sub> &#x0003d; 1</td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">f<sub>i</sub>&#x003ba;<sub>Endog</sub> &#x0003d; 40&#x02013;90</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">[Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>MT,rest</sub> &#x0223c; 0 nM</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left"><inline-formula><tex-math id="M67">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\tilde {V}}_{{\mathrm{max}},SERCA}=2.1\hspace{.167em}{\mathrm{{\mu}M}}\hspace{.167em}{\mathrm{s}}^{-1}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">B<sub>total,fura</sub> &#x0003d; 50&#x02013;90 &#x003bc;M</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">&#x003ba;<sub>MT</sub> &#x0003d; 4,000</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">EC<sub>50,SERCA</sub> &#x0003d; 30 nM</td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">K<sub>d,Fura</sub> &#x0003d; 224 nM</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">v<sub>MT</sub>/v<sub>Cell</sub> &#x0003d; 0.01</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">n<sub>SERCA</sub> &#x0003d; 2.5</td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">D<sub>Endog</sub> &#x0003d; 0&#x02013;15 &#x003bc;m<sup>2</sup>s<sup>&#x02212;1</sup></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left" valign="top"/><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">&#x003ba;<sub>ER</sub> &#x0003d; 20</td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">D<sub>Ca</sub> &#x0003d; 300 &#x003bc;m<sup>2</sup>s<sup>&#x02212;1</sup></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left" valign="top"/><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">v<sub>ER</sub>/v<sub>Cell</sub> &#x0003d; 0.04</td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">D<sub>Fura</sub> &#x0003d; 50 &#x003bc;m<sup>2</sup>s<sup>&#x02212;1</sup></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left" valign="top"/><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left" valign="top"/></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">v<sub>Cell</sub> &#x0003d; 35 pL</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left" valign="top"/><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left" valign="top"/></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left">[Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>i,rest</sub> &#x0223c; 50&#x02013;100 nM</td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left" valign="top"/><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" align="left" valign="top"/></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap><p>The equations describing Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> removal are consistent with the finding that in sympathetic neurons treated with Tg and subjected to blockade of the Na/Ca exchanger, the rate of Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> removal at each instant in time during the recovery depends on the [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>i</sub> level at that time. Thus, under these conditions, the time dependence of (J<sub>Extru</sub>&#x0002b;J<sub>uni</sub>) during the recovery is derived from the time dependence of [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>i</sub>:<disp-formula id="fda13"><label>(A13)</label><tex-math id="M68">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}J_{Extru}(t)+J_{uni}(t)=J_{Extru}([Ca^{2+}]_{i}(t))+J_{uni}([Ca^{2+}]_{i}(t)).\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula></p></sec><sec><title>Mitochondrial Na<sup>&#x0002b;</sup>/Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> Exchanger: J<sub>NaCa</sub></title><p>To study mitochondrial Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> release via the Na<sup>&#x0002b;</sup>/Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> exchanger, cells were stimulated under the same conditions as in Case 3 but with pipette solutions containing added Na<sup>&#x0002b;</sup>. <inline-formula><tex-math id="M69">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\tilde {J}}_{i}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula> now includes a fourth component (<inline-formula><tex-math id="M70">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\tilde {J}}_{NaCa}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula>):<disp-formula id="fda14"><label>(A14)</label><tex-math id="M71">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{gather*}\frac{d[Ca^{2+}]_{i}}{dt}=-\frac{{\tilde {J}}_{VSCC}+{\tilde {J}}_{Extru}+{\tilde {J}}_{uni}+{\tilde {J}}_{NaCa}}{v_{Cell}(f_{i}{\kappa}_{i})} \\\frac{d[Ca^{2+}]_{MT}}{dt}=\frac{{\tilde {J}}_{uni}+{\tilde {J}}_{NaCa}}{v_{MT}{\kappa}_{MT}}.\end{gather*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula></p><p>The equation for d[Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>i</sub>/dt can be rewritten as follows:<disp-formula id="fda15"><label>(A15)</label><tex-math id="M72">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}\frac{d[Ca^{2+}]_{i}}{dt}=- \left \left[\frac{I_{Ca}}{2Fv_{Cell}(f_{i}{\kappa}_{i})}+(J_{Extru}+J_{uni}+J_{NaCa})\right] \right ,\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>where<disp-formula id="fda16"><label>(A16)</label><tex-math id="M73">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}J_{NaCa}=\frac{{\tilde {J}}_{NaCa}}{v_{Cell}(f_{i}{\kappa}_{i})}.\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula></p><p>Analysis of J<sub>NaCa</sub> and its regulation by Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> is more complicated than J<sub>Extru</sub> or J<sub>uni</sub> because <inline-formula><tex-math id="M74">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\tilde {J}}_{NaCa}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula> depends on the intramitochondrial Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> concentration ([Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>MT</sub>), which was not directly measured in this study. However, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib8">Colegrove et al. (2000b)</xref> described an approach to measuring a quantity that can be directly related to [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>MT</sub>, as long as [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>MT</sub> is proportional to the total concentration, which is assumed here. This approach makes use of the net mitochondrial Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> flux (J<sub>MT</sub>). With information about the time course of J<sub>MT</sub>, it is possible to obtain information about the dynamics of intramitochondrial Ca concentration. The starting point is provided by the following equation:<disp-formula id="fda17"><label>(A17)</label><tex-math id="M75">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{gather*}[Ca^{2+}]_{MT}(t)=[Ca^{2+}]_{MT}(0)+{\Delta}[Ca^{2+}]_{MT}(t) \\=[Ca^{2+}]_{MT}(0)+{\int _{0}^{t}}\frac{{\tilde {J}}_{MT}(t)}{v_{MT}{\kappa}_{MT}}dt,\end{gather*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>where [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>MT</sub>(t) is the mitochondrial Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> concentration at time t, [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>MT</sub>(0) is the resting level of [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>MT</sub>, and &#x00394;[Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>MT</sub>(t) is the difference between [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>MT</sub>(t) and [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>MT</sub>(0). As shown below, integration of J<sub>MT</sub> provides a measure of intramitochondrial Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> levels during the recovery that can be used to characterize the functional dependence of J<sub>Na/Ca</sub> on [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>MT</sub>. If mitochondrial Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> buffering is fast and low affinity and can be described by a constant buffering factor &#x003ba;<sub>MT</sub>, then [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>MT</sub> can be related to measured quantities as follows:<disp-formula id="fda18"><label>(A18)</label><tex-math id="M76">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{gather*}[Ca^{2+}]_{MT}(t)=[Ca^{2+}]_{MT}(0)+{\int _{0}^{t}}\frac{{\tilde {J}}_{MT}(t)}{v_{MT}{\kappa}_{MT}}dt \\=[Ca^{2+}]_{MT}(0)-{\int _{t}^{{\infty}}}\frac{{\tilde {J}}_{MT}(t)}{v_{MT}{\kappa}_{MT}}dt \\=[Ca^{2+}]_{MT}(0)-{\int _{t}^{{\infty}}}J_{MT}(t)\frac{v_{i}{\kappa}_{i}([Ca^{2+}]_{i})}{v_{MT}{\kappa}_{MT}}dt \\=[Ca^{2+}]_{MT}(0)-\frac{v_{i}}{v_{MT}{\kappa}_{MT}}{\int _{t}^{{\infty}}}J_{MT}(t){\kappa}_{i}([Ca^{2+}]_{i})dt \\=[Ca^{2+}]_{MT}(0)+\frac{v_{i}}{v_{MT}{\kappa}_{MT}}{\Delta}[Ca^{2+}]_{MT}^{(0)}(t),\end{gather*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>where &#x00394;[Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>MT</sub><sup>(0)</sup>(t) is the negative integral of J<sub>MT</sub>&#x003ba;<sub>i</sub> from t to &#x0221e;, and the negative integral from t to &#x0221e; has been substituted for the integral from 0 to t, making use of <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="fda19">Eq. A19</xref>, which is valid as long as stimulus-evoked changes in [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>MT</sub> are reversible (i.e., if [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>MT</sub> eventually returns to its resting level in the aftermath of a stimulus):<disp-formula id="fda19"><label>(A19)</label><tex-math id="M77">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{gather*}0={\int _{0}^{{\infty}}}\frac{{\tilde {J}}_{MT}(t)}{v_{MT}{\kappa}_{MT}}dt \\={\int _{0}^{t}}\frac{{\tilde {J}}_{MT}(t)}{v_{MT}{\kappa}_{MT}}dt+{\int _{t}^{{\infty}}}\frac{{\tilde {J}}_{MT}(t)}{v_{MT}{\kappa}_{MT}}dt.\end{gather*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula></p><p>An analogous approach was used to evaluate changes in ER Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> levels influencing the driving force for Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> release via RyRs (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib2">Albrecht et al., 2002</xref>). To describe the relationship between J<sub>Na/Ca</sub> and [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>MT</sub> we used the following equation:<disp-formula id="fda20"><label>(A20)</label><tex-math id="M78">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}J_{NaCa}([Ca^{2+}]_{MT})=-\frac{{\tilde {V}}_{{\mathrm{max}},NaCa}}{ \left \left[1+\displaystyle\frac{EC_{50,NaCa}}{[Ca^{2+}]_{MT}}\right] \right {\kappa}_{i}}.\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>Substituting for [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>MT</sub> from <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="fda18">Eq. A18</xref> gives a relationship between the measured quantities J<sub>NaCa</sub>, &#x003ba;<sub>i</sub>, and &#x00394;[Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>MT</sub><sup>(0)</sup>(t):<disp-formula id="fda21"><label>(A21)</label><tex-math id="M79">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{gather*}J_{NaCa}([Ca^{2+}]_{MT}(t))= \\-\frac{{\tilde {V}}_{{\mathrm{max}},NaCa}}{ \left \left[1+ \left \left[\displaystyle\frac{EC_{50,NaCa}}{[Ca^{2+}]_{MT}(0)+\displaystyle\frac{v_{i}}{v_{MT}{\kappa}_{MT}}{\Delta}[Ca^{2+}]_{MT}^{(0)}(t)}\right] \right \right] \right {\kappa}_{i}}.\end{gather*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>This equation includes four parameters, <inline-formula><tex-math id="M80">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\tilde {V}}_{{\mathrm{max}},NaCa}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula>, EC<sub>50,NaCa</sub>, [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>MT</sub>(0), and v<sub>i</sub>/(v<sub>MT</sub>&#x003ba;<sub>MT</sub>), but can be converted to an equation with three lumped parameters that is more convenient for comparison with data:<disp-formula id="fda22"><label>(A22)</label><tex-math id="M81">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{gather*}J_{NaCa}([Ca^{2+}]_{MT}(t))= \\-\frac{{\tilde {V}}_{{\mathrm{max}},NaCa}}{ \left \left[1+ \left \left[\displaystyle\frac{ \left \left\{EC_{50,NaCa}\displaystyle\frac{v_{MT}{\kappa}_{MT}}{v_{i}}\right\} \right }{ \left \left\{[Ca^{2+}]_{MT}(0)\displaystyle\frac{v_{MT}{\kappa}_{MT}}{v_{i}}\right\} \right +{\Delta}[Ca^{2+}]_{MT}^{(0)}(t)}\right] \right \right] \right {\kappa}_{i}} \\=-\frac{P_{3}}{ \left \left[1+ \left \left[\displaystyle\frac{P_{4}}{P_{5}+{\Delta}[Ca^{2+}]_{MT}^{(0)}(t)}\right] \right \right] \right {\kappa}_{i}},\end{gather*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>where<disp-formula id="fda23"><label>(A23)</label><tex-math id="M82">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{gather*}P_{3}={\tilde {V}}_{{\mathrm{max}},NaCa} \\P_{4}=EC_{50,NaCa}\frac{v_{MT}{\kappa}_{MT}}{v_{i}} \\P_{5}=[Ca^{2+}]_{MT}(0)\frac{v_{MT}{\kappa}_{MT}}{v_{i}}.\end{gather*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula></p><p>We plotted J<sub>NaCa</sub> vs. &#x00394;[Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>MT</sub><sup>(0)</sup>(t) and fit the data with <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="fda22">Eq. A22</xref> to obtain estimates of P<sub>3</sub>&#x02013;P<sub>5</sub>, treating this equation as a function of two independent variables, &#x00394;[Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>MT</sub><sup>(0)</sup> and &#x003ba;<sub>i</sub>([Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>i</sub>), that depend parametrically on time. Given (&#x003ba;<sub>MT</sub>v<sub>MT</sub>)/v<sub>i</sub> and the parameters P<sub>4</sub> and P<sub>5</sub>, estimates of EC<sub>50,NaCa</sub> and [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>MT</sub>(0) can be obtained by dividing the parameters by (&#x003ba;<sub>MT</sub>v<sub>MT</sub>)/v<sub>i</sub>. The latter calculation gave an initial value of [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>MT</sub> for simulations.</p></sec><sec><title>ER Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> Uptake and Release: J<sub>SERCA</sub>, J<sub>RyR</sub></title><p>To include contributions from ER Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> transport, we used descriptions of Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> uptake via SERCAs (J<sub>SERCA</sub>) and release (J<sub>Release</sub>) from <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib2">Albrecht et al. (2002)</xref> and inserted these into <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="fdm1">Eqs. M1</xref>&#x02013;<xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="fdm3">M3</xref>.<disp-formula id="fda24"><label>(A24)</label><tex-math id="M83">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\tilde {J}}_{SERCA}([Ca^{2+}]_{i}(t))=\frac{{\tilde {V}}_{{\mathrm{max}},SERCA}}{ \left \left[1+\displaystyle\frac{EC_{50,SERCA}}{[Ca^{2+}]_{i}}\right] \right ^{n_{SERCA}}}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula><disp-formula id="fda25"><label>(A25)</label><tex-math id="M84">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\tilde {J}}_{{\mathrm{Re}}lease}([Ca^{2+}]_{i},[Ca^{2+}]_{ER})={\tilde {P}}_{ER}([Ca^{2+}]_{i}-[Ca^{2+}]_{ER}),\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>where<disp-formula id="fda26"><label>(A26)</label><tex-math id="M85">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\tilde {P}}_{ER}={\tilde {P}}_{ER,Basal}+\frac{{\tilde {P}}_{{\mathrm{max}},RyR}}{ \left \left[1+\displaystyle\frac{EC_{50,RyR}}{[Ca^{2+}]_{i}}\right] \right ^{n_{RyR}}}.\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>The parameters of J<sub>SERCA</sub> have the conventional meanings. <inline-formula><tex-math id="M86">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\tilde {P}}_{ER}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula> describes the Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> permeability of the ER, which consists of a constant, Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>-insensitive component (<inline-formula><tex-math id="M87">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\tilde {P}}_{ER,Basal}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula>), and a Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>-sensitive component that represents the permeability imparted to the ER by ryanodine receptors (RyRs). The latter component has a maximal value <inline-formula><tex-math id="M88">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}{\tilde {P}}_{{\mathrm{max}},RyR}\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></inline-formula>, [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>i</sub> for half maximal activation EC<sub>50, RyR</sub>, and cooperativity factor n<sup>RyR</sup>.</p></sec><sec><title>Diffusion Model</title><p>We developed a radial model of Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> diffusion in a spherical cell consisting of multiple concentric shells and a central sphere (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib38">Sala and Hern&#x000e1;ndez-Cruz, 1990</xref>; <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib34">Nowycky and Pinter, 1993</xref>; see also <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib9">Connor and Nikolakopoulou, 1982</xref>). Calcium enters the cell across the plasma membrane and diffuses into the interior shells while also being transported by the organelles within each shell. We adopted the fast-buffer approximation (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib25">Keener and Sneyd, 1998</xref>), which assumes that buffering occurs instantaneously, which is reasonable given the long time scales and continuous stimuli we are considering (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib41">Strier et al., 2003</xref>). Using this simplification, diffusion leads to changes in Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> concentration in the n<sup>th</sup> shell ([Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>i,n</sub>) described by <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="fda27">Eq. A27</xref>:<disp-formula id="fda27"><label>(A27)</label><tex-math id="M89">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{gather*}\frac{{\partial}[Ca^{2+}]_{i,n}}{{\partial}t}= \\\frac{(D_{Ca}+D_{Endog}{\kappa}_{Endog}+D_{Fura}{\kappa}_{Fura})}{1+{\kappa}_{Endog}+{\kappa}_{Fura}}{\nabla}^{2}[Ca^{2+}]_{i,n} \\-\frac{2D_{Fura}{\kappa}_{Fura}}{1+{\kappa}_{Endog}+{\kappa}_{Fura}}{\vert}{\nabla}[Ca^{2+}]_{i,n}{\vert}^{2},\end{gather*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>where D<sub>Ca</sub>, D<sub>Endog</sub>, and D<sub>Fura</sub> are the diffusion coefficients of cytoplasmic Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>, the endogenous buffer, and fura-2, respectively; Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> transport by organelles is described by additional terms that are not shown for simplicity. Within each shell, the concentration of free and total calcium in organelles was assumed to be spatially uniform, and direct intraluminal diffusion between shells was not allowed. The gradient was calculated by taking radially symmetric derivatives. Our measurements of cytoplasmic buffering strength provided estimates of P<sub>1</sub> &#x0003d; f<sub>i</sub>(1 &#x0002b; &#x003ba;<sub>Endog</sub>) and P<sub>2</sub> &#x0003d; f<sub>i</sub>B<sub>total,fura</sub>, which were used to describe buffering in the uniform model (see <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="fdm10">Eq. M10</xref>). For the diffusion model, descriptions of &#x003ba;<sub>Endog</sub> and &#x003ba;<sub>Fura</sub> were needed. Instead of estimating f<sub>i</sub>, which based on a combined mitochondrial and ER volume fraction of &#x0223c;0.05 would give f<sub>i</sub> &#x0223c; 0.95, and calculating &#x003ba;<sub>Endog</sub> and &#x003ba;<sub>Fura</sub>, we used the measured parameters directly (equivalent to setting f<sub>i</sub> &#x0003d; 1), which may lead to an underestimation of the buffering parameters by &#x0223c;5%. The similarity between calculations based on the diffusion model (with f<sub>i</sub> set to unity) and the uniform model (which uses the measured values P<sub>1</sub> and P<sub>2</sub> and makes no assumptions about f<sub>i</sub>) when I<sub>Ca</sub> is small argues that this approximation does not introduce appreciable error.</p><p>We integrated the model numerically in a spherically symmetric coordinate system using a partially implicit approach. In this method, the diffusion rates are calculated using the concentration at the next time step, while the fluxes are calculated using the calcium concentration at the previous time step. To check our model, we first made sure that with I<sub>Ca</sub> &#x0003d; 0, resting [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>i</sub> was steady in all shells. As another check, we made sure calcium was conserved during diffusion. During all of our simulations we calculated the total calcium concentration in the cell in two ways: first by adding the number of moles of calcium in the cytoplasm and all organelles, and second by calculating the starting concentration and then integrating all of the fluxes entering and leaving the cell. We found that these two calculations were within 1% of each other for the diffusion parameters we used, and that this error could be reduced with smaller time and space steps.</p><p>Each shell included two organellar compartments that occupy a constant volume fraction of that shell. Since all fluxes were measured based on the impact of the corresponding transport pathway on [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>i</sub>, to describe the impact of these fluxes on organellar Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> levels, we multiplied the equations describing organelle transport by v<sub>Cell</sub>f<sub>i</sub>&#x003ba;<sub>i</sub>/v<sub>MT</sub>&#x003ba;<sub>MT</sub> (for mitochondria) and v<sub>Cell</sub>f<sub>i</sub>&#x003ba;<sub>i</sub>/v<sub>ER</sub>&#x003ba;<sub>ER</sub> (for ER). f<sub>i</sub>&#x003ba;<sub>i</sub> was obtained from cytoplasmic Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup> buffering measurements, and the lumped parameters v<sub>Cell</sub>/v<sub>MT</sub>&#x003ba;<sub>MT</sub> and v<sub>Cell</sub>/v<sub>ER</sub>&#x003ba;<sub>ER</sub> were calculated based on estimates of volume fraction (v<sub>MT</sub>/v<sub>Cell</sub>, v<sub>ER</sub>/v<sub>Cell</sub>) and buffering strength (&#x003ba;<sub>MT</sub>, &#x003ba;<sub>ER</sub>).</p></sec><sec><title>Simulated Fluorescence-based [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>i</sub> Measurements</title><p>We based fluorescence simulations on those of <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib29">McHugh and Kenyon (2004)</xref>. In brief, we calculated the fluorescence arising from the <italic>n</italic>th shell at two excitation wavelengths:<disp-formula id="fda28"><label>(A28)</label><tex-math id="M90">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}F_{350,n}=S_{f,350}([Fura]_{n})+S_{b,350}[CaFura]_{n}),\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula><disp-formula id="fda29"><label>(A29)</label><tex-math id="M91">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}F_{380,n}=S_{f,380}([Fura]_{n})+S_{b,380}[CaFura]_{n}),\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>where [Fura] and [CaFura] are the concentrations of free and bound fura-2, and S<sub>f,350</sub> &#x0003d; 4.97, S<sub>f,380</sub> &#x0003d; 11.3, S<sub>b,350</sub> &#x0003d; 11, S<sub>b,380</sub> &#x0003d; 1 are parameters describing contributions to fluorescence intensity from free and bound forms of fura-2 satisfying the experimentally determined relations R<sub>max</sub> &#x0003d; (S<sub>b,350</sub>/S<sub>b,380</sub>) &#x0003d; 11, R<sub>min</sub> &#x0003d; (S<sub>f,350</sub>/S<sub>f,380</sub>) &#x0003d; 0.44, and (S<sub>f,380</sub>/S<sub>b,380</sub>) &#x0003d; 11.3. The concentration of bound fura-2 in the <italic>n</italic>th compartment ([CaFura]<sub>n</sub>) is:<disp-formula id="fda30"><label>(A30)</label><tex-math id="M92">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}[CaFura]_{n}=\frac{[Ca^{2+}]_{i,n}B_{total,fura}}{K_{d,Fura}+[Ca^{2+}]_{i,n}},\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>where K<sub>d,Fura</sub> &#x0003d; 224 nM and B<sub>total,fura</sub> is the total concentration of fura-2. We calculated the concentration of unbound fura-2 by subtracting [CaFura]<sub>n</sub> from B<sub>total,fura</sub>, which is assumed to be constant in all shells. From F<sub>350,n</sub> and F<sub>380,n</sub> we calculated the fluorescence ratio in each shell R<sub>n</sub> &#x0003d; F<sub>350,n</sub>/F<sub>380,n</sub>, which can be directly converted to [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>i,n</sub> by the equation (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib18">Grynkiewicz et. al., 1985</xref>):<disp-formula id="fda31"><label>(A31)</label><tex-math id="M93">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}[Ca^{2+}]_{i,n}=K_{D,Fura} \left \left(\frac{R_{n}-R_{{\mathrm{min}}}}{R_{{\mathrm{max}}}-R_{n}}\right) \right \frac{S_{f,380}}{S_{b,380}}.\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula></p><p>Average [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>i</sub> ([Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>i,avg</sub>) was calculated in two ways (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="bib29">McHugh and Kenyon, 2004</xref>). For the first approach,<disp-formula id="fda32"><label>(A32)</label><tex-math id="M94">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}[Ca^{2+}]_{i,avg}=\frac{{{\sum^{N}_{n=0}}}[Ca^{2+}]_{i,n}vol_{n}}{vol_{total}}.\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>For the second approach, the total fluorescence was calculated at each of the two excitation wavelengths by summing the fluorescence from each shell, and the ratio was calculated:<disp-formula id="fda33"><label>(A33)</label><tex-math id="M95">\documentclass[10pt]{article}\usepackage{amsmath}\usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy}\usepackage{mathrsfs}\usepackage{pmc}\usepackage[Euler]{upgreek}\pagestyle{empty}\oddsidemargin -1.0in\begin{document}\begin{equation*}R_{avg}=\frac{{\sum}_{n=0}^{N}(S_{f,350}([Fura]_{n})+S_{b,350}[CaFura]_{n})vol_{n}}{{\sum}_{n=0}^{N}(S_{f,380}([Fura]_{n})+S_{b,380}[CaFura]_{n})vol_{n}}.\end{equation*}\end{document}</tex-math></disp-formula>R<sub>avg</sub> was then inserted into <xref ref-type="disp-formula" rid="fda31">Eq. A31</xref> to give [Ca<sup>2&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>i,avg</sub>.</p></sec></app></app-group></back></article>